Porous ionic liquids or liquid metal-organic frameworks ?
\(^{1}\)Institut de Chimie de Clermont-Ferrand, CNRS and Université Clermont Auvergne, BP80026, Aubiere Cedex F-63171, France. \(^{2}\)Department of Physical Chemistry, University of Chemistry and Technology, Technická 5, Praha 6, CZ-16628, Czech Republic.
Liquids with permanent porosity can be created by incorporating empty cavities into liquid phases hence adding novel functionalities to conventional fluid absorbents and opening new perspectives for a range of applications including separations, catalysis and drug delivery.\(^{[1,2]}\) The preparation of porous liquids is difficult, especially in the case where they consist of a neat fluid composed by molecular entities containing empty cavities (type I porous liquid\(^{[3]}\)) or when these molecular entities are dissolved in sterically hindered solvents (type II porous liquids\(^{[2,3]}\)). Amongst the synthetic approaches proposed so far, the dispersion and stabilisation of solid porous materials in hindered solvents, too large to enter the pores of the solid allowing the formation of type III porous liquids, has not been considered. We have prepared a new porous liquid from the dispersion of well characterized metal-organic frameworks in an appropriately chosen ionic liquid. Both ionic liquids and metal-organic frameworks are new families of materials, now easily prepared and available as off-the-shelf chemicals whose fascinating properties have already lead to applications in engineering, physics, chemistry and medicine\(^{[4,5]}\). Porous liquids prepared by the addition of only 2 to 5% of ZIF-8 to a phosphonium based ionic liquid are capable of absorbing reversibly large quantities of gas when compared with the pure ionic liquid (+63% of carbon dioxide, +100% of methane and +150% of nitrogen). The suspension of the solid material is stable for several months while reversibly maintaining its high gas absorption capacity.
Porous materials are usually solid, their structural rigidity allowing the presence of cavities of defined sizes and shapes. Porous solids are often used in adsorption-based processes but they can present disadvantages when compared with liquid absorption routes, related with mass transport difficulties and unfavourable thermodynamics. The recuperation of the absorbent can in turn be difficult because its regeneration involves large energy consumptions related with the molecular interactions between the dissolved species. The incorporation of cavities in liquids\(^{[2]}\) is an appealing alternative to the use of porous solids or absorbent liquids opening the possibility of exploring the advantages of both in different applications.
The concept of porous liquids has been introduced in 2007\(^{[3]}\) with their classification as\(^{[1]}\): type I porous liquids are pure fluids containing empty cavities, while type II and type III are dissolved empty hosts or homogeneously dispersed framework materials in hindered solvents, respectively. Liquids with permanent porosity have been synthesised by extending the chain length of molecular cages\(^{[6]}\) or dispersing microporous frameworks in organic solvents\(^{[7]}\). But the fabrication of porous fluids has been challenging because intermolecular self-filling, collapse or decomposition of the organic hosts with serious settling of nanoparticles can be observed during their preparation\(^{[8]}\). Noteworthy successes have been reported in the preparation of type I and type II \({}^{[2]}\) porous liquids: in the first case, a porous liquid has been prepared based on hollow silica spheres following a two-step synthetic procedure and in the second case a cage molecule has been dissolved in a bulky solvent that could not penetrate the pores.
Our goal is to prepare type III porous liquids using easily available, well characterised materials like metal-organic framework (MOFs) and ionic liquids (ILs). Metal-organic frameworks or porous coordination polymers are a class of crystalline organic-inorganic hybrid porous materials built by coordination of metal clusters or ions with organic linkers\(^{[9]}\). They can be synthesized incorporating a wide variety of structures and functions when compared to other porous solid materials, such as zeolites. Even if these solids exhibit a considerable flexibility, their crystalline structures and porosities are regular and can be easily characterised. Ionic liquids are salts of bulky, flexible and asymmetric organic cations and anions that are liquids near ambient temperature. All ILs share several noteworthy properties – low vapour pressure, wide liquidus range, relatively high viscosity and a
remarkable capability of dissolving various organic or inorganic materials. Besides, they can be tuned to improve one or several of their properties by combining appropriate ions or by functionalising their organic moieties. ILs have been incorporated into the pores of MOFs to form composite materials showing superior performances in applications such as gas storage, adsorption and membrane-based gas separation, catalysis and ionic conductivity[11,12]. So far, only a limited number of the thousands of existing MOFs and hundreds of ILs synthesized to date have been used to prepare composite materials, the incorporation of the IL in the porous structure being done at temperatures often too high to guarantee the thermal stability of the liquid[10].
Gravimetric gas solubility measurements of carbon dioxide, methane and nitrogen were performed in the liquid suspensions at a constant temperature and as a function of pressure to probe the free volume. The suspensions are capable of reversibly absorbing much larger quantities of the three gases than the pure IL (Fig. 2b, 2c and 2d). For example, at 303 K and 5 bar, the molar absorption of carbon dioxide per mass of liquid increases \(63\%\) for the \(5\%\) w/w suspension of ZIF-8 (Fig. 2b) while, in the same conditions, the absorption of methane or nitrogen increases by more than \(100\%\) (Fig. 2c and 2d). This result can only be explained by the presence of the ZIF-8 pores that remain void in the suspension in \(\left[\mathsf{P}_{6,6,6,14}\right]\left[\mathsf{N T f}_{2}\right]\), which can then be considered as a type III porous liquid.
