In MOFs ZIF-8 and HKUST-1 (also known as \(\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}\) or MOF-199, a porous solid with \(9\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) main channels and tetrahedral-shaped \(5\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) side-pockets accessible through \(3\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) triangular windows\[19\]), simulations show that the ions do not occupy the pores in the solid (Fig. 3a and Fig. 3c), although alkyl side chains of the phosphonium cations are seen inside the surface-accessible pores. On the other hand, the cylindrical pores of Mg-MOF-74 are filled with liquid as depicted in the snapshot of Fig. 3e. These simulation results are in full agreement with experiment for ZIF-8 and Mg-MOF-74, HKUST-1 was not studied here experimentally.
Although the atomistic models used in classical molecular simulations contain simplifications with respect to details of molecular interactions, molecular volumes and conformations are well reproduced. The main information sought here from simulation
Fig. 3b, 3d and 3f allow the visualisation of the free volumes for the ZIF-8, HKUST-1 and Mg-MOF-74 suspensions in \(\left[\mathsf{P}_{6,6,6,14}\right]\left[\mathsf{N t f}_{2}\right]\), respectively. The cavities present in the entire systems in the simulation snapshots, are given by the contact accessible volume for a test void radius of 1.75 Å. Some spontaneous cavities are seen distributed in the liquid regions in all systems, but the liquid-free cavities present in the slabs of ZIF-8 and HKUST-1 are evident, in contrast to Mg-MOF-74.
We show that stable suspensions of MOFs in ILs can form porous liquids capable of reversibly absorbing large quantities of gas. Molecular simulation is a valuable tool to study the composite systems at the molecular scale and to identify appropriate MOF-IL pairs. Preparing porous liquids from MOFs and from ILs allows us to benefit from the structural and chemical variety and tuneability of these two classes of materials. The ions of the IL can be chosen for chemical compatibility with the MOFs insuring that the dispersed particles are stable. The molecular volume of the IL has to be matched with the size of the accessible cavities of the MOF, determined by the organic linkers in the framework. Here ILss offer a specific advantage when compared to molecular solvents: because it is energetically prohibitive to de-solvate one type of ion (due to the coulombic cohesive energy), both ions do not need to be larger than the cavities; effectively the smallest unit
of an ionic liquid that is removable from the bulk liquid would be at least an ion pair.
